### Performance and resource utilization improvements

- #15618 Batch updates to hashes using shared templates now run more efficiently
- #15018 Supported x86 systems now use the processor-based monotonic clock by default, with runtime calibration to reduce timing overhead
- #15782 RDB and AOF loading now provides sequential-read hints to the operating system to improve loading efficiency
- RediSearch/RediSearch#10169 Searches on indexes using field-level expiration (`HEXPIRE`) are faster. (MOD-16350)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#10981, RediSearch/RediSearch#11065 Coordinator field lookup and row-reply serialization overhead is reduced. (MOD-17924)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#11055, RediSearch/RediSearch#11056, RediSearch/RediSearch#11057 Cluster reply conversion, result destruction, and `GROUPBY` cleanup have reduced overhead. (MOD-17908, MOD-17909, MOD-17910)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#11507, RediSearch/RediSearch#11506 Query replies now format integral numeric values without `snprintf` and declare reply collection lengths up front. (MOD-18800, MOD-18799)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#11509 Hash field loading for `RETURN` and `LOAD` no longer requires per-field allocations. (MOD-18811)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#10246 Concurrent writes are now allowed during vector range queries. (MOD-16437)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#10515 Coordinator requests are now allocated on the main thread, removing `CoordRequestCtx`. (MOD-16990)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#11018 Query usage is now separated from administrative index operations. (MOD-17536)
- RediSearch/RediSearch#10066 Shard response barrier removed in favor of async iterator depletion callback for `FT.AGGREGATE WITHCOUNT`. (MOD-16210)
- RedisJSON/RedisJSON#1645 More accurate memory usage reporting for shared strings: Memory usage now accounts for each reference’s proportional share of a shared string’s allocation, including its header and padding, instead of charging the full allocation to every reference
- J1660 Reduced object memory consumption: JSON parsing now uses compact object allocation to reduce the actual memory needed to store JSON objects 
- RedisJSON/RedisJSON#1661 Improved JSONPath parsing and evaluation by reducing parser backtracking, temporary allocations, and repeated traversal. Optimizations include caching root-path operands across filter candidates, stopping existence checks at the first match, and reducing overhead in regex matching and string comparisons
- RedisJSON/RedisJSON#1631 Improved typed-array element access through direct indexing, avoiding repeated iteration and quadratic work during full indexed scans 
- RedisBloom/RedisBloom#1049 Improved t-digest compression performance for inputs containing many duplicate values. Sorting is now bounded to O(n log n), avoiding quadratic behavior on pathological inputs

### Configuration parameters

- Redis-roaring:`bitmap-default-roaring` - to enable the new roaring bitmaps type, disabled by default 
- RedisJSON/RedisJSON#1657 Configurable RDB depth validation: Added `json-legacy-rdb-depth-limit`, disabled by default, to enforce a maximum nesting depth of 128 when loading JSON from RDB or through RESTORE
- RedisJSON/RedisJSON#1640 Added`json-auto-create-deep-paths` and is disabled by default and can only be configured at load time. Use the same value on every node of a shard and when replaying AOF files, because differing values can cause data divergence

